brachial plexopathy through potential anatomical adaptations and repetitive strain mechanisms."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's anabolic steroid use leading to muscle hypertrophy plausibly explain thoracic outlet compression as the cause of his neurovascular symptoms?", "summary": "Muscle hypertrophy from anabolic steroid use strongly supports thoracic outlet syndrome as a potential explanation given the anatomical risk factors."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented trauma in a non-acute presentation disfavor traumatic brachial plexopathy despite bodybuilding activities and steroid use?", "summary": "The absence of documented trauma moderately disfavors traumatic brachial plexopathy, even in the context of bodybuilding and steroid use."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of positional variability in symptoms (no change with head/neck position) disfavor thoracic outlet syndrome despite the bodybuilder's anatomic risks?", "summary": "The absence of positional changes in symptoms provides minimal evidence against thoracic outlet syndrome, consistent with its anatomical plausibility but not definitive."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.355], ["B3", 0.245], ["B2", 0.233]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.128], ["B2", 0.09], ["B3", 0.084]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.123], ["B2", 0.089], ["B3", 0.08]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch posterior (0.08959) below confirmation threshold and no dangerous alternative (danger >=0.7, posterior >=0.10) remains unresolved. Tree depth (2/3) allows further expansion."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.32, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.08863494674668235, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'thoracic outlet syndrome', 'Brachial plexus neuropathy', 'Neck muscle weakness', 'brachial plexopathy', 'Abnormality of the brachial nerve plexus'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.355}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Thoracic Outlet Syndrome", "posterior": 0.089, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 3, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "The absence of documented trauma moderately disfavors traumatic brachial plexopathy, even in the context of bodybuildin…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nThoracic Outlet Syndrome vs Traumatic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the ribs, arthralgia, edema, emg abnormality, muscle spasm\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nThoracic Outlet Syndrome vs Neoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (e.g., Pancoast Tumor):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the ribs, arthralgia, edema, emg abnormality, muscle spasm\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nRelated to Thoracic Outlet Syndrome: arterial thoracic outlet syndrome, neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome, syndromic disease\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: Case report: Thoracic outlet syndrome in an elite archer in full-draw position. > Prognosis\n  Overall, the prognosis is excellent in patients with thoracic outlet syndrome. Patients who undergo conservative therapy have their symptoms resolve in about 90% of cases. Most of these individuals do\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.72):]\n  Source: Radiation-induced brachial plexus neuropathy: A review. > Clinical Significance\n  Thoracic outlet syndrome occurs as a result of the compression of the lower trunk and subclavian vessels. Common causes include a cervical rib, which is an extra rib arising from the C7 vertebra in le\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.63):]\n  Source: Radiation-induced brachial plexus neuropathy: A review. > Clinical Significance\n  Thoracic outlet syndrome occurs as a result of the compression of the lower trunk and subclavian vessels. Common causes include a cervical rib, which is an extra rib arising from the C7 vertebra in le\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Thoracic Outlet Syndromes. > Clinical Significance\n  Thoracic Outlet Syndrome\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → anatomical compression from repetitive strain/muscle hypertrophy → Thoracic Outlet Syndrome\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for anatomical abnormalities (e.g., scalene muscle hypertrophy, cervical rib) in thoracic outlet structures due to chronic bodybuilding strain\n  He drinks 2-7 alcoholic drinks per day and has smoked 2 packs of cigarettes per day since he was 25. He has lost 17 pounds since he last came to the emergency department 1 month ago. → apical lung tumor (Pancoast tumor) with paraneoplastic effects → Apical Lung Tumor (Pancoast Tumor) with Brachial Plexus Compression\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Consider Pancoast tumor as the underlying cause of brachial plexus symptoms given his smoking history and unexplained weight loss\n  The patient uses anabolic steroids → steroid-induced muscle imbalance or structural compression → Thoracic Outlet Syndrome\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess for muscle hypertrophy patterns that could compress neurovascular structures at the thoracic outlet\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
